data.content:
Here are the three EF Core 10 breaking changes affecting your code and how to fix each:

---

## 1. `ExecuteUpdateAsync` — now accepts a regular lambda

The API changed from `Expression<Func<...>>` to `Action<...>`. Your manual expression tree construction no longer compiles, but can be dramatically simplified:

```csharp
// .NET 10 — simple lambda with conditionals
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(s =>
{
    s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
    if (nameChanged)
    {
        s.SetProperty(b => b.Name, "foo");
    }
});
```

No more `Expression.Lambda`, `Expression.Call`, etc.

---

## 2. Complex type column naming changes (two issues)

**Same-named properties** across complex types now get uniquified column names (e.g., `Street` → `Street1`). **Nested complex types** use the full path (`Complex_NestedComplex_Property` instead of `NestedComplex_Property`).

Both generate schema-altering migrations. Fix by pinning column names explicitly:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, ab => {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingAddress_Street");
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, ab => {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_Street");
    });
});

// For nested: Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, pb => {
        pb.ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress, ab => {
            ab.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_City");
        });
    });
});
```

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `logCommandText` parameter

All methods gained a `string logCommandText` parameter (redacted SQL for safe logging):

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        string logCommandText) // ← new parameter
    {
        // Use logCommandText for logging (constants may be redacted)
        // Use command.CommandText for actual SQL
    }
}
```

---

**Summary:** The first change simplifies your code significantly. The second requires explicit `HasColumnName()` calls to prevent unwanted migrations. The third is a one-line signature addition per method.
